===== api =====
REST + GraphQL client for the terminal

Usage:
  api [command]

Available Commands:
  again       Repeat the Nth most recent GET request (default: the last one)
  config      Show and edit the global / project config (envs, defaults, aliases)
  del         DELETE a path
  diff        Send the same request to two envs and show how the JSON differs
  doctor      Check the setup: binary, shell shim, completion, config, session, tools, .env permissions
  env         Show, load or clear the API session (API_BASE_URL, API_TOKEN, ...)
  form        POST multipart (-F) or urlencoded (-d) form data
  get         GET a path
  gql         GraphQL request against $API_GRAPHQL_URL (or $API_BASE_URL/graphql)
  headers     GET a path and print only the response headers
  history     List recent requests (method, path, filter: never bodies or tokens)
  json        Build a JSON object from key=value / key:=json arguments
  login       Log in (REST or GraphQL) and store the token as API_TOKEN
  logout      Forget this shell's token and the cached token of the env
  openapi     Index an OpenAPI/Swagger JSON spec for completion (sync, list)
  patch       PATCH with a JSON body
  post        POST a JSON body
  put         PUT a JSON body
  run         Run saved requests from a .http file (JetBrains / VS Code REST Client format)
  schema      Explore a GraphQL service by introspection (names, types, local cache)
  secret      Store passwords in the system credential store (macOS Keychain)
  shell-init  Print the shell integration (login, env load/use/clear, completion)
  time        Request timing breakdown: dns / connect / tls / ttfb / total
  watch       GET a path every interval until Ctrl+C

Flags:
      --base string         base URL (default $API_BASE_URL)
      --color string        colour output: auto, always or never (env API_COLOR, NO_COLOR, JQ_COLORS) (default "auto")
  -C, --color-output        always colour the output (jq -C)
      --debug               print the equivalent curl command to stderr (or set API_DEBUG=1)
      --env string          use the [env.<name>] profile from config (see: api config show)
  -M, --monochrome-output   never colour the output (jq -M)
      --no-pager            never page long output (env API_PAGER sets the pager, empty disables)
      --token string        bearer token (default $API_TOKEN)

Use "api [command] --help" for more information about a command.

===== api again =====
Repeats a request from the history. Only GET requests can be repeated: bodies
and tokens are never stored, so a POST would be sent without its body. The
current env and token are used, so after switching envs this runs there. A
.filter argument replaces the stored one. All the usual flags (-i, -c, --dry,
...) work. --pick chooses the entry with fzf.

Usage:
  api again [N] [.filter] [--pick] [flags]

Examples:
  api again            # repeat the last request
  api again 3 .data.id # the third most recent, with a different filter
  api again --pick

Flags:
      --columns strings            with --table: the columns to show, in order (a,b,c)
  -c, --compact                    compact JSON output
      --connect-timeout duration   connect time limit (env CURL_CONNECT_TIMEOUT, seconds) (default 10s)
      --dry                        print the curl command instead of sending
      --har string                 also write the exchange to a HAR file (appends; secrets are masked)
      --har-secrets                with --har: keep tokens, cookies and passwords in the file (dangerous to share)
  -H, --header stringArray         extra header, "Name: value" (repeatable)
  -i, --include                    print the status line and response headers
  -k, --insecure                   skip TLS certificate verification
      --no-auth                    do not send the bearer token
  -o, --output string              write the body to a file
      --pick                       choose the entry with fzf
  -Q, --query stringArray          query parameter key=value (repeatable)
  -R, --raw-body                   print the body untouched: no filter, no pretty-print
  -r, --raw-output                 print string results without quotes (jq -r)
      --retry int                  retry transport errors and 502/503/504 N times
      --summary                    print a status/type/time/size footer to stderr (env API_SUMMARY)
  -t, --table                      print an array of objects (or one object) as a table
      --timeout duration           total time limit (env CURL_MAX_TIME, seconds) (default 5m0s)
      --trust-hosts                send your token to an absolute URL on another host (default: only to the active base URL)
  -v, --verbose                    print request and response headers to stderr

Global Flags:
      --base string         base URL (default $API_BASE_URL)
      --color string        colour output: auto, always or never (env API_COLOR, NO_COLOR, JQ_COLORS) (default "auto")
  -C, --color-output        always colour the output (jq -C)
      --debug               print the equivalent curl command to stderr (or set API_DEBUG=1)
      --env string          use the [env.<name>] profile from config (see: api config show)
  -M, --monochrome-output   never colour the output (jq -M)
      --no-pager            never page long output (env API_PAGER sets the pager, empty disables)
      --token string        bearer token (default $API_TOKEN)

===== api config =====
Optional TOML config. Global: ~/.config/api/config.toml. Project: the nearest
.api.toml. They merge key by key (project wins).

  api --env dev get /users       use [env.dev]
  api me                         run the alias [alias] me = "..."

It never holds secrets: tokens and passwords are not part of the schema, and a
profile can only name a variable (token_env = "API_TOKEN_DEV") that holds one.
Treat a project .api.toml like code: it chooses the server your requests go to.

Usage:
  api config [command]

Available Commands:
  edit        Open the config file in $EDITOR (created from the template if missing)
  get         Print one effective value (e.g. env.dev.url, defaults.timeout)
  init        Create a commented config file
  path        Show which config files are in use
  schema      Print the JSON Schema of the config files (for editors and CI)
  set         Set a value in the project file (if there is one) or the global file
  show        Show the effective (merged) config
  validate    Check config files for errors and for settings that will be ignored

Global Flags:
      --base string         base URL (default $API_BASE_URL)
      --color string        colour output: auto, always or never (env API_COLOR, NO_COLOR, JQ_COLORS) (default "auto")
  -C, --color-output        always colour the output (jq -C)
      --debug               print the equivalent curl command to stderr (or set API_DEBUG=1)
      --env string          use the [env.<name>] profile from config (see: api config show)
  -M, --monochrome-output   never colour the output (jq -M)
      --no-pager            never page long output (env API_PAGER sets the pager, empty disables)
      --token string        bearer token (default $API_TOKEN)

Use "api config [command] --help" for more information about a command.

