===== api json =====
key=value is a string, key:=<json> is raw JSON (number, bool, null, array,
object). Dotted keys nest ({"a":{"b":1}} from a.b:=1) unless --flat is set.

A string value can be a reference, so a password never appears in the command
line: password=@keychain:NAME, @env:NAME or @prompt (@@x is the literal "@x").

Usage:
  api json key=value... [flags]

Examples:
  api json name=cat age:=30 admin:=true tags:='["a","b"]'
  api json user.name=cat user.id:=1 | api post /users

Flags:
  -c, --compact   one-line output
      --flat      keep dots in keys instead of nesting

Global Flags:
      --base string         base URL (default $API_BASE_URL)
      --color string        colour output: auto, always or never (env API_COLOR, NO_COLOR, JQ_COLORS) (default "auto")
  -C, --color-output        always colour the output (jq -C)
      --debug               print the equivalent curl command to stderr (or set API_DEBUG=1)
      --env string          use the [env.<name>] profile from config (see: api config show)
  -M, --monochrome-output   never colour the output (jq -M)
      --no-pager            never page long output (env API_PAGER sets the pager, empty disables)
      --token string        bearer token (default $API_TOKEN)

===== api login =====
Send credentials, pull the token out of the response with a jq path and
export it as API_TOKEN.

With no arguments, the login of the selected env is used: the [env.<name>.login]
block of your global config, with credentials taken from value references
(@keychain:NAME, @env:NAME, @prompt). Set it up once, then just run "api login":

  api secret set upscale-dev        # asks once, stores in the Keychain
  api config set env.dev.login.gql 'mutation($input: LoginInput!) { login(input: $input) { accessToken } }' --global
  api config set env.dev.login.endpoint v2/auth --global
  api config set env.dev.login.vars.input.username me@x.com --global
  api config set env.dev.login.vars.input.password @keychain:upscale-dev --global
  api login --env dev

REST (default with a path): POST <path> with a JSON body; token path defaults to .data.token.
GraphQL (--gql): send a login mutation like "api gql" does (same query / @file /
stdin / -V handling, -e endpoint); token path defaults to .data.login.accessToken.
-V values accept references too: -V input.password=@keychain:NAME.

A child process cannot change its parent shell, so --emit prints the export
statement for the shell to eval; the zsh "api" function does that for you.
No bearer token is sent on the login call: a stale one can turn it into a 401.
--dry shows the request with references unresolved and sends nothing.

Usage:
  api login [<path> [body] | --gql <mutation>] [.token-path] [flags]

Examples:
  api login                                  # configured login of the current env
  api login --env thien
  api login /auth/login '{"email":"x","password":"y"}' '.data.access_token'
  api json email=x password=@keychain:dev | api login /auth/login '.data.access_token'
  api login --gql -e v2/auth @login.graphql -V input.username=me@x.com -V input.password=@prompt

Flags:
      --columns strings             with --table: the columns to show, in order (a,b,c)
  -c, --compact                     compact JSON output
      --connect-timeout duration    connect time limit (env CURL_CONNECT_TIMEOUT, seconds) (default 10s)
  -d, --data string                 request body; @file reads a file, - reads stdin
      --dry                         print the curl command instead of sending
      --emit export API_TOKEN=...   print export API_TOKEN=... for the shell to eval
  -e, --endpoint string             (--gql) path appended to the GraphQL base, or a full URL
      --gql                         GraphQL login: send a mutation instead of a REST body
      --har string                  also write the exchange to a HAR file (appends; secrets are masked)
      --har-secrets                 with --har: keep tokens, cookies and passwords in the file (dangerous to share)
  -H, --header stringArray          extra header, "Name: value" (repeatable)
  -i, --include                     print the status line and response headers
  -k, --insecure                    skip TLS certificate verification
      --no-auth                     do not send the bearer token
  -o, --output string               write the body to a file
  -Q, --query stringArray           query parameter key=value (repeatable)
  -R, --raw-body                    print the body untouched: no filter, no pretty-print
  -r, --raw-output                  print string results without quotes (jq -r)
      --retry int                   retry transport errors and 502/503/504 N times
      --summary                     print a status/type/time/size footer to stderr (env API_SUMMARY)
  -t, --table                       print an array of objects (or one object) as a table
      --timeout duration            total time limit (env CURL_MAX_TIME, seconds) (default 5m0s)
      --trust-hosts                 send your token to an absolute URL on another host (default: only to the active base URL)
  -V, --var stringArray             variable key=value or key:=json (repeatable); values may be @keychain:NAME / @env:NAME / @prompt
  -v, --verbose                     print request and response headers to stderr

Global Flags:
      --base string         base URL (default $API_BASE_URL)
      --color string        colour output: auto, always or never (env API_COLOR, NO_COLOR, JQ_COLORS) (default "auto")
  -C, --color-output        always colour the output (jq -C)
      --debug               print the equivalent curl command to stderr (or set API_DEBUG=1)
      --env string          use the [env.<name>] profile from config (see: api config show)
  -M, --monochrome-output   never colour the output (jq -M)
      --no-pager            never page long output (env API_PAGER sets the pager, empty disables)
      --token string        bearer token (default $API_TOKEN)

===== api logout =====
Unsets API_TOKEN in this shell and deletes the token cached for the env
(defaults.token_cache). With --env NAME, only that env's cached token and its
token_env variable are forgotten; this shell's API_TOKEN stays unless NAME is
the env the shell is on. --all forgets the cached token of every env in your
config. Tokens cached for envs that are no longer in your config can be removed
with: api secret rm token.<env>

Like login, it changes your shell: run it through the zsh "api" function.

Usage:
  api logout [--env NAME] [--all] [flags]

Flags:
      --all    forget the cached token of every env in the config
      --emit   print shell statements for eval

Global Flags:
      --base string         base URL (default $API_BASE_URL)
      --color string        colour output: auto, always or never (env API_COLOR, NO_COLOR, JQ_COLORS) (default "auto")
  -C, --color-output        always colour the output (jq -C)
      --debug               print the equivalent curl command to stderr (or set API_DEBUG=1)
      --env string          use the [env.<name>] profile from config (see: api config show)
  -M, --monochrome-output   never colour the output (jq -M)
      --no-pager            never page long output (env API_PAGER sets the pager, empty disables)
      --token string        bearer token (default $API_TOKEN)

===== api openapi =====
Reads an OpenAPI 3 or Swagger 2 document (JSON) from the server and keeps a small
index of its method + path pairs, so "api get <TAB>" completes the endpoints the API
really has. Completion stays offline: it reads that one local file.

  api openapi sync               # the env's openapi path, else tries /openapi.json,
                                 # /swagger.json, /v3/api-docs, /api-docs, /api-json, /docs-json
  api openapi sync /docs/spec.json
  api openapi sync --file ./openapi.json
  api openapi list users
  api openapi                    # pick an operation, edit the request, save and run it (like api gql)

Usage:
  api openapi [flags]
  api openapi [command]

Available Commands:
  list        List the cached operations (filter matches path, summary or operationId)
  pick        Pick an operation with fzf, edit the request, save and run it
  show        Show one cached operation (summary and body skeleton): the picker's preview
  sync        Fetch the spec and cache its index for the active server

Flags:
      --columns strings            with --table: the columns to show, in order (a,b,c)
  -c, --compact                    compact JSON output
      --connect-timeout duration   connect time limit (env CURL_CONNECT_TIMEOUT, seconds) (default 10s)
      --dry                        print the curl command instead of sending
      --har string                 also write the exchange to a HAR file (appends; secrets are masked)
      --har-secrets                with --har: keep tokens, cookies and passwords in the file (dangerous to share)
  -H, --header stringArray         extra header, "Name: value" (repeatable)
  -i, --include                    print the status line and response headers
  -k, --insecure                   skip TLS certificate verification
      --no-auth                    do not send the bearer token
      --no-save                    do not keep the request under .api/requests/
  -o, --output string              write the body to a file
      --print                      print the api command for the picked operation: no editor, nothing sent
  -Q, --query stringArray          query parameter key=value (repeatable)
  -R, --raw-body                   print the body untouched: no filter, no pretty-print
  -r, --raw-output                 print string results without quotes (jq -r)
      --retry int                  retry transport errors and 502/503/504 N times
      --summary                    print a status/type/time/size footer to stderr (env API_SUMMARY)
  -t, --table                      print an array of objects (or one object) as a table
      --timeout duration           total time limit (env CURL_MAX_TIME, seconds) (default 5m0s)
      --trust-hosts                send your token to an absolute URL on another host (default: only to the active base URL)
  -v, --verbose                    print request and response headers to stderr
      --yes                        do not ask before sending a request that changes data

Global Flags:
      --base string         base URL (default $API_BASE_URL)
      --color string        colour output: auto, always or never (env API_COLOR, NO_COLOR, JQ_COLORS) (default "auto")
  -C, --color-output        always colour the output (jq -C)
      --debug               print the equivalent curl command to stderr (or set API_DEBUG=1)
      --env string          use the [env.<name>] profile from config (see: api config show)
  -M, --monochrome-output   never colour the output (jq -M)
      --no-pager            never page long output (env API_PAGER sets the pager, empty disables)
      --token string        bearer token (default $API_TOKEN)

Use "api openapi [command] --help" for more information about a command.

===== api openapi list =====
List the cached operations (filter matches path, summary or operationId)

Usage:
  api openapi list [filter] [flags]

Flags:
      --method string   only this HTTP method

Global Flags:
      --base string         base URL (default $API_BASE_URL)
      --color string        colour output: auto, always or never (env API_COLOR, NO_COLOR, JQ_COLORS) (default "auto")
  -C, --color-output        always colour the output (jq -C)
      --debug               print the equivalent curl command to stderr (or set API_DEBUG=1)
      --env string          use the [env.<name>] profile from config (see: api config show)
  -M, --monochrome-output   never colour the output (jq -M)
